# dist/ is committed build output, and its BYTES are hashed: payloadHash in
# scripts/payload.ts is a content hash over dist/chrome, dist/chrome/loader and
# the xpi, recorded per binary in installer/bin/payload-state.json and checked by
# `npm run check`. It has to hash exact bytes — those are what ships — which
# makes line-ending conversion a correctness problem, not a cosmetic one.
#
# This repo develops on Windows with core.autocrlf=true, so without this file
# every `git checkout` rewrote the committed (LF) bundles to CRLF in the working
# tree. The result was a `npm run check` that failed immediately after any branch
# switch — "built from an older payload" for all six installers, "staged payload
# is out of date: data/chrome/userChrome.uc.js differs from dist/chrome/
# userChrome.uc.js" — with no source edit behind it, and only `npm run build`
# (which rewrites dist as LF) clearing it. CI on Linux, where autocrlf is off,
# never saw it.
#
# -text means "never convert": git writes these blobs verbatim and compares them
# byte-for-byte, so the working tree matches what was committed on every
# platform, and the recorded hash keeps describing the tree. It also keeps the
# binaries under dist/ (the xpi, the icons) safe from a `text` attribute, which
# would try to normalize them.
dist/** -text

# The same rule for the installer's //go:embed staging copy. `npm run check`
# compares these files byte-for-byte against dist/ ("staged payload is out of
# date: data/loader/config.js differs from dist/chrome/loader/config.js"), and
# the tracked loader files here are text: with core.autocrlf=true git wrote them
# with CRLF while dist — marked -text just above — stayed LF. Same blob, different
# bytes, so the check failed on a clean checkout with nothing edited, and only a
# rebuild that rewrote dist as CRLF cleared it. These are build inputs whose bytes
# are the point, exactly like dist/ itself: never convert them.
installer/internal/payload/data/** -text

# NOT added here on purpose, and worth knowing before someone tries: payloadHash()
# also hashes native-host/**/*.{go,mod,sum,json} RAW, so the recorded payload in
# payload-state.json depends on those files' line endings too. Marking them -text
# makes a Windows checkout agree with Linux CI — but it INVALIDATES every hash
# already recorded (the committed state was written with CRLF sources here), so it
# is only correct together with a rebuild commit that refreshes all six binaries
# and the state file. A lone attribute change turns six fresh binaries into six
# stale ones; see docs/RELEASING.md.
